A||N||3||Warhawk||starting problem||||1||
Z||000000||Warhawk||05-03-2000||11:58 AM||||I've got an electrical problem I need help with. My '86 Fiero will not start, won't even turn over. I am picking up a shop manual tonight, but I'm wondering if anyone here can help. I've taken a voltage tester and checked for voltage at the solenoid and I'm not getting anything. I bypassed the clutch cancel switch, still nothing. I also checked for voltage at the clutch cancel switch, nothing. I'm thinking it may be the ignition switch, but it does kill the radio, etc. when turned to the start position. I wonder if there is a relay or a fuseable link or something that I am missing.  I pulled the starter and had it checked, it's fine. Any ideas?||||reg||1
Z||000001||Oreif||05-03-2000||12:54 PM||||There are a few fuseable links and fuses in the engine compartment. Do you have 12V to the starter? The battery should connect to the starter. It's the other side of the starter that goes to the clutch switch and ingnition switch. Do you get power to the ECM? You can check fuses under dash too. You also did not mention if you checked the battery. A bad plate/cell could cause the voltage to drop below 9V when you draw a lot of current. Like when you try to engage starter. Since its a manual have you tried to "push start" it? <p>[This message has been edited by Oreif (edited 05-03-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||Warhawk||05-03-2000||02:14 PM||||The battery is good, and I have voltage to the starter. It's the circuit to the solenoid that is dead. I bump started the car to get it home, it fired right up.  Everything else works just fine. I stopped by the parts store and picked up an ignition switch, the actual electrical switch, not the lock cylinder.  From looking at the wiring diagram this appears to be about the only thing left. For $9 it's worth a try.||||reg||1
Z||000003||theogre||05-03-2000||02:58 PM||||check the clutch interlock switch?||||reg||1
